\f1\fs22 The explosion, the tank, a squad of Elvi in camouflage ponchos\'97all GI Blues series by the look of them\'97everything moved too fast for Chanta. Out of the rubble of the studio, he was escorted between two rows of more Elvi standing in oddly synchronised judo poses, into the back of an aging Cadillac that had seen better decades and worse drivers.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The trip was fast, the kind that would make a risk assessor weep into their spreadsheet. They pulled up at ornate gates caging an expansive, battered lawn around a colonial mansion. The Cadillac door opened and, without being told, Chanta got out.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>An honour guard brought Chanta up the drive\'97clearly a tourist trap the Cloudborne seemed to love. The building was parched for paint, and the lawns chopped up as if a stampede of Shetland ponies had square-danced through. At the portico, the Elvi stopped. Chanta stopped.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A gleaming white town car and three suburbans came up the drive at regally slow pace. Crisp-suited professionals climbed out, followed by a woman who looked like Hollywood royalty with a weapons license. Her entourage swept past Chanta\'92s guards and disappeared inside. One Elvis gestured: \'93Go inside. Wait in the lobby.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>When the Hollywood weapons dealer called Chanta in, he had no idea what, or who, was behind the door.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Tony!\'94 Sarah cried, her voice weak. To Chanta, Sarah appeared somehow diminished\'97like a deflated balloon animal from a particularly depressing children\'92s party.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Hollywood actress said something to Tony\'97nothing Sarah caught\'97then left, her entourage streaming after like expensive lemmings.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta, expecting a hug, found the floor instead. Sarah\'92s fist met his face in a blur, and the tiles cradled his cheek with all the tenderness of concrete.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He prodded the growing dent. \'93What the tax, Sarah? I think you broke my cheekbone.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah shook out her hand, exhaling fury. \'93Never mind. I\'92m sure you\'92ll be good as new in a couple of hours. Like a bloody wind-up toy.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta pushed himself up, poking at his bruised face. \'93What are you talking about?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Every day for a month, I\'92ve watched them torture you on broadcast. And every day you bounce back, pink as if you\'92d just come from a manicure, a sauna, and a motivational seminar.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Yeah, I\'92ve been feeling like a colt lately, apart from the daily\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah shoved him into the couch. \'93Ever since you raped me while I was unconscious!\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What? I never\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Don\'92t lie to me! That night, after the Vegas Show, you took advantage of me while I was drunk and defenceless!\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Sarah, I swear,\'94 Chanta pleaded, cowering. \'93I\'92d never hurt you. I passed out straight away. Slept like the dead.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Maybe you attacked me and just don\'92t remember.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I was completely smashed. Out cold all night. I know it.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah sniffled, hunting for somewhere to hang her anger. \'93Then why\'92d you slink off in the morning?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I went to get you coffee. Got nabbed while I was out.\'93\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93That doesn\'92t explain\'85\'94 She gestured at his existence in general. \'93You!\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Tony, I can\'92t have a baby. Ever.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta made a thinking face. \'93You got a pox or something?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Yes, Tony, I got a taxing pox.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You\'92re making no sense.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Look at you! Goons have been beating you like a pi\'f1ata for weeks, and you keep coming back mint condition.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You watched?\'94 Chanta asked, perversely proud.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93It didn\'92t matter how they sliced, diced, or tenderised you\'97next day you were shrink-wrapped fresh.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I think I know what caused this.\'93\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah grabbed his chin, terrified. \'93No, you don\'92t.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93It\'92s that disc.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah stopped cold. \'93It can\'92t be.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Sarah, I swear I never touched you. I may be Cloudborne, but I\'92m not that kind of Cloudborne.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Then how am I pregnant?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta made a what-the-tax face which was quickly painted over by a how-should-I-know face. Then he pieced a theory together. \'93Come on, Sarah. I saw The Johnny leaving with Jane Fonda\'92s weapons-dealing granny.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Johnny?\'93 Sarah blinked. \'93Johnny Stoat? He\'92d be the last man I\'92d sleep with. The man thinks foreplay is a PowerPoint presentation.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta looked both confused and hopeful: perhaps he still had a chance with Sarah. A spark struck and lit up dormant parts of his brain. \'93Those Sages! \'91A child by Christmas.\'92 That\'92s what the creepy one said.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah was unclear which of the decrepit old rolls of mouldy carpet was noticeably creepier than the others.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93They seemed committed to casting you into that prophecy of theirs.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>It was the obvious conclusion, especially now; oddly, Sarah believed Tony\'92s denial. Her blood chilled. She felt like a King-Kong-sized mitt had grabbed her and was waving her at some angry bi-planes. She always feared crazy people would come looking for her sooner or later. \'93That still doesn\'92t explain why you look so good.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta beamed. \'93You think I look good?\'94\

\f2\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>In a career criminal kind of way
\f1\i0 , Sarah thought. \'93I mean, how are not burned and beaten?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I dunno. I stopped taking fibre pills when they turned out to be shredded carpet underlay.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah\'92s stomach fluttered.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta lit up, like he\'92d had a profound thought. \'93It\'92s the disc. Think about it: everything has been going haywire since I\'85 \'94 Chanta repressed visions of the blue-suited corpse on the street. \'93\'85 found it. It\'92s been nothing but trouble. It makes the ground shake, it crashes vans, and it nearly buried itself in your\'85\'92 he waved in the general co-ordinates of Sarah\'92s breasts. \'93Then it glued itself to my fingers. It\'92s like it\'92s alive, one time scaly like a crocodile, then next time all slobbery like a hungry, hungry\'85\'94 And that\'92s where the wattage of Chanta\'92s brain failed him.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Crocodile,\'94 Sarah said as she dropped onto the couch beside him.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Sarah, you didn\'92t keep it, did you?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah looked into her hands.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Tell me you left it where I threw it.\'93\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I had it, but I just gave it away.\'93\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta recoiled. \'93To who? Don\'92t tell me The Johnny.\'93\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah nodded. \'93I think he\'92ll study it.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta wrestled with his jealousy. \'93Well, that\'92s... good?\'93\

\f2\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Wonderful,
\f1\i0  Sarah thought. 
\f2\i I\'92ve just handed infected biotech that flips vehicles and molests people to Ulyssia\'92s premier weapons manufacturer.
